    <div class="content-container">
        <div class="text-content">
            <h2 class="section-title">Legal and Compliance Framework for Copyright Objection</h2>
            <div class="content-text">
                
                <p><strong>Copyright Act 1957 - Sections 44-50A:</strong> Section 44 establishes Register. Section 45 allows registration applications. Section 47 provides 30-day waiting period. Section 48 empowers Registrar to examine and raise objections. Source: <a href="https://www.copyright.gov.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">copyright.gov.in</a></p><p><strong>30-Day Objection Window:</strong> Application published in Copyright Journal. Any person may file objection within 30 days of publication.</p><p><strong>Discrepancy Letter:</strong> Registrar examines for compliance. Issues letter citing concerns. Applicant has 30 days to reply. Non-reply = rejection.</p><p><strong>Hearing Provisions:</strong> If written reply does not resolve, Registrar schedules hearing. Both parties present arguments. Quasi-judicial decision.</p><p><strong>Appeal (Post-IPAB):</strong> Writ petitions to High Court. For Pune: High Court of Bombay at Pune has jurisdiction.</p><p><strong>Eastern Book v D.B. Modak (2008):</strong> Supreme Court standard - copyright requires modicum of creativity, not mere labour. Applies to all objection assessments.</p><p><strong>Copyright Rules 2013 (amended 2021):</strong> Electronic filing. Modernised deposit requirements for computer software.</p>

            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

